WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:07.382
It's one of the first
things we look for,

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:08.842
when searching for new life.

00:00:09.176 --> 00:00:11.136
And it's essential
to sustaining our own.

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:15.015
We know there's water on Mars.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:17.935
But without much
atmospheric pressure,

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:19.728
♪♪

00:00:22.231 --> 00:00:24.149
It doesn't behave
the way you think.

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:26.401
I'm Wrenn Schmidt,

00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:28.237
and we're exploring
the science behind

00:00:28.237 --> 00:00:29.238
"For All Mankind."

00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:37.246
Because water is essential
to a future on Mars,

00:00:37.246 --> 00:00:38.288
we wanted to explore

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:40.374
what it would take
for Ed, Kelly

00:00:40.374 --> 00:00:42.125
and the Mars
crew to actually

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:44.253
extract water
on the red planet.

00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:46.463
[clicks]

00:00:47.381 --> 00:00:49.466
Let's start with Mars' surface.

00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:52.469
It's not particularly...

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:53.971
habitable.

00:00:54.972 --> 00:00:56.890
Water requires
pressure to stay liquid.

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.476
Unlike Earth who's atmosphere

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:00.894
creates the pressure necessary

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:02.562
to give us lakes and oceans.

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:05.691
Mars barely has an atmosphere.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.734
♪♪

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:10.279
So, it's surface is
devoid of liquid water.

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:17.744
Ice and gas can exist on
the low pressure surface,

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:19.913
but to find water in it's liquid

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:21.456
life sustaining form,

00:01:21.832 --> 00:01:23.083
you'll have to dig deep...

00:01:27.254 --> 00:01:28.463
where the weight of the ground

00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:30.007
above creates more pressure.

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:32.718
And it's this
difference in pressure,

00:01:32.718 --> 00:01:34.303
above versus below.

00:01:34.678 --> 00:01:36.847
That makes extracting
that water tricky.

00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:39.474
As the liquid can
turn suddenly,

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:41.518
and dangerously to steam.

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:47.024
Welcome to Valles Marineris.

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.943
The Grand Canyon of Mars.

00:01:50.694 --> 00:01:52.487
Where scientists have
recently discovered

00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:53.864
high levels of hydrogen.

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:55.699
It's here..

00:01:55.699 --> 00:01:56.908
that in the show the Soviets

00:01:56.908 --> 00:01:58.618
detect a large under ground lake

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.329
using ground penetrating radar

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:02.205
and team up with
Helios to drill.

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:06.668
Ed and the team know
that the deeper they drill,

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:08.545
the more pressure
they'll need underground.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.132
If they don’t match this
increasing pressure,

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:14.051
the water will
convert to steam,

00:02:14.051 --> 00:02:16.136
expand and shoot up
through the hole.

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:19.473
So they pump CO2
into the drill casing

00:02:19.473 --> 00:02:21.058
maintaining a
pressure equilibrium.

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:23.977
But when they fail
to maintain that balance

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.687
and the drill
penetrates the reservoir.

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:27.564
The drop in pressure makes

00:02:27.564 --> 00:02:29.232
all that water turn to steam

00:02:29.232 --> 00:02:30.442
and rush up.

00:02:30.484 --> 00:02:31.485
[sound of steam pipe]

00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:33.403
[sound of explosion]

00:02:35.906 --> 00:02:37.699
NASA's official Mars strategy

00:02:37.699 --> 00:02:38.992
is to follow the water.

00:02:39.493 --> 00:02:41.578
If we're going to make
life there viable for us,

00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:42.954
we'll need a water source.

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:44.414
We'll need to maintain the

00:02:44.414 --> 00:02:46.166
proper balance
when we access it.

00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:47.793
And make
sure we aren't

00:02:47.793 --> 00:02:49.378
disturbing another life,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.172
potentially thriving
in the water

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.673
just beneath the surface.

00:02:53.799 --> 00:02:55.300
♪♪

